IMPORT prelude
-- A ledger row is forced for display under the temporal context its write
-- was made in, not under the directive's root context.
--
-- `snap` is written under EVAL UNDER VALID TIME 2023-06-01 and holds a
-- RECALL of `rate`, still unevaluated at the write. As of that day `rate`
-- is 7 (the 9 is only valid from 2024). The #EVAL's own result forces the
-- RECALL under the clause and says 7. Before the fix the "Ledger:" block
-- forced the same RECALL at the root, where no valid time is pinned, and
-- printed 9 on a row stamped vt=2023-06-01 — the row and the result
-- disagreed, with no diagnostic. Now the row says what the result says.
--
-- The test harness pins the clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z.

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"

DECLARE Box
  HAS v IS A MAYBE NUMBER

DECLARE Out
  HAS a IS A NUMBER
      b IS A NUMBER
      c IS A Box

-- Result: Out OF 7, 9, (Box OF (JUST OF 7)); the `snap` row: Box OF (JUST OF 7).
#EVAL Out
        (`EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2023) (RECORD `rate` IS 7))
        (`EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2024) (RECORD `rate` IS 9))
        (`EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 6 2023) (RECORD `snap` IS (Box (RECALL `rate`))))

-- The whole context is restored for the row, not only the valid time: a
-- row written under RULES EFFECTIVE AT 2023-06-01 shows that date, which
-- is what a bare #EVAL of `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` under the same clause
-- returns. Forced at the root it fell back to the clock, 2025-01-31.
--
-- The Result's own field still reads 2025-01-31: the clause's deep pin does
-- not reach a nullary reader nested as a constructor field. That is so on
-- the binary before this fixture too, and is not what this fixture guards.
DECLARE Dated
  HAS on IS A DATE

#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 6 2023)
        (RECORD `when` IS (Dated `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`))
